                                        "entry_id": "e2e16492-6f24-3ddf-bc9e-144c3ffa9a90",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A diacritical mark ⟨˜⟩ placed above a letter to modify its pronunciation.",
                                            "Another name for apex, a curved diacritic used in the 17th century to mark final nasalization in the early Vietnamese alphabet. It was an adoption of the Portuguese tilde."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "1412cd2b-ab09-3219-833f-6b9df6321d37",
                                        "entry_id": "e2e16492-6f24-3ddf-bc9e-144c3ffa9a90",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A symbol ⟨~⟩, with various names and uses, also known as swung dash or wave dash. In the computer industry, various other names may be used, such as squiggle and twiddle."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "883f57c2-9268-47bf-862a-00b1232199ff",
                                                "sense_id": "1412cd2b-ab09-3219-833f-6b9df6321d37",
                                                "text": "swung dash A stock keyboard character, used in mathematics as the sign of similarity (a ~ b) and in lexicography as a sign of repetition. The same sign has been used in symbolic logic to indicate negation, but to avoid confusion, the angular negation symbol (¬) is preferred. Not to be confused with the tilde."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
